{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790955957,"nonce":"ntik4fXm0HcQBTUnCBcbbjY8dow=","title":"A Wren↔Python import cycle exits the process and hangs instead of loading or failing","message":"Repro (hosted, interop fixtures): bench/python_tally.py does `from bench.tally import Tally`; add `import \"bench:python_tally\" for PythonTally` to bench/tally.wren, then have Haxe call a PythonTally static (the interop bench's Haxe→Python column). The process stops making progress at 0% CPU and never returns.\n\nSampled stack, outermost first: Haxe import entry → registry::lookup_class_or_load(PythonTally) → caribou_zyntax::load(python_tally) → zyntax_python parse → Linker::link_imports → note_host(bench.tally) → caribou_python::host_module → registry::resolve_or_load → caribou_wren::project::load(tally) → VM::interpret → load_import(\"bench:python_tally\") → caribou_wren import resolver → registry::resolve_or_load → caribou_zyntax::load(python_tally) again, while its first load is still on the stack → the Zyntax HIR interpreter → call_native_through → exit() → thread-local destructors → dropping the RefCell\u003cHashMap\u003c_, caribou_zyntax::State\u003e\u003e, where it blocks.\n\nSo a module that is mid-load is loaded again re-entrantly, something on that path calls exit(), and exit then deadlocks in a TLS destructor. Python allows cyclic imports (the second import sees the partly initialised module), and Wren allows them too; either the cycle should resolve to the module being loaded, or the load should fail with an error naming the cycle. Neither exit nor a hang is acceptable. The interop bench avoids the cycle by putting its Wren→Python callers in a module of their own (bench/to_python.wren).","files":null}]}